WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.509 --> 00:00:15.729
We are about to tell you a story that you
might think you know...but unless you know

00:00:15.729 --> 00:00:20.298
that the Ku Klux Klan was involved...you’re
going to want to stick around.

00:00:20.298 --> 00:00:28.388
Because the interface of humanity and the
natural world is always a very fraught place.

00:00:28.388 --> 00:00:32.989
Pets are a matter of personal preference,
but they’re also a bit about the branding.

00:00:32.989 --> 00:00:38.099
Like, we all know that there’s kind of a
feel to a dog person and a cat person.

00:00:38.099 --> 00:00:40.479
But also, take brine shrimp.

00:00:40.479 --> 00:00:46.779
That’s not a name that suggests a particularly
exciting pet, but that did not stop me.

00:00:46.779 --> 00:00:51.958
When I was seven years old my dad helped me
follow the instructions on an ad in, I think,

00:00:51.958 --> 00:00:58.639
a teenage mutant ninja turtles comic so that
I could order my very own brine shrimp.

00:00:58.639 --> 00:01:01.299
Of course, they were not called brine shrimp.

00:01:01.299 --> 00:01:03.179
They were called sea monkeys.

00:01:03.179 --> 00:01:07.149
And they were, I’ll be honest, pretty difficult
to take care of.

00:01:07.149 --> 00:01:09.448
I don’t think they lasted very long.

00:01:09.448 --> 00:01:15.659
Now, not everyone had sea monkeys, just like
not everyone watched the 1992 sitcom “The

00:01:15.659 --> 00:01:21.189
Amazing Live Sea Monkeys” about three human-sized
sea monkeys and the professor (played by Howie

00:01:21.189 --> 00:01:23.169
Mandel) who enlarged them.

00:01:23.169 --> 00:01:29.488
In fact, given that the show lasted only 11
episodes, I don’t know that anyone watched it.

00:01:29.488 --> 00:01:34.528
But that’s okay because we don’t need
a professor to enlarge our sea monkeys.

00:01:34.528 --> 00:01:41.089
We have a microscope.

00:01:41.089 --> 00:01:45.479
But before we get to the organism behind all
of this magic, let’s talk a little bit about

00:01:45.479 --> 00:01:53.288
the magic itself—the experience of “instant
life” (as it was marketed) packaged into a toy.

00:01:53.288 --> 00:01:58.819
The premise is kind of immense, like you feel
as if you are creating life, but the instructions

00:01:58.819 --> 00:02:00.019
were simple.

00:02:00.019 --> 00:02:05.219
All you needed, according to the original
1972 patent, were three packets.

00:02:05.219 --> 00:02:11.169
Packet 1 contained the water conditioner,
Packet 2 contained the eggs, and Packet 3

00:02:11.169 --> 00:02:12.499
contained the food.

00:02:12.499 --> 00:02:18.049
Just add Packet 1 to get the water ready,
wait 24 hours, then add Packet 2…and voila,

00:02:18.049 --> 00:02:22.029
watch in amazement as they instantly hatch.

00:02:22.029 --> 00:02:27.008
Now the “instant life” of the sea monkey
is actually the achievement less of you or

00:02:27.008 --> 00:02:33.589
the people at Sea Monkey HQ, and more of Artemia
salina, the brine shrimp.

00:02:33.589 --> 00:02:39.359
While not technically a shrimp, the brine
shrimp is a crustacean found in salty lakes and ponds.

00:02:39.359 --> 00:02:43.839
And while their rebranding as sea monkeys
is a more recent innovation, Artemia have

00:02:43.839 --> 00:02:47.368
been around for over 100 million years.

00:02:47.368 --> 00:02:52.579
Artemia swim upside-down, and breathe through
gills near the base of their limbs.

00:02:52.579 --> 00:02:57.109
And they spend their days seeking out their
favorite food: algae.

00:02:57.109 --> 00:03:01.729
But Artemia also make for great food themselves,
which is why they’re cultivated to feed

00:03:01.729 --> 00:03:06.648
fish being farmed for your own future meals
or aquariums.

00:03:06.648 --> 00:03:11.368
The Artemia’s downfall, if you want to call
it that, is its own resilience.

00:03:11.368 --> 00:03:14.368
Female Artemia can produce two types of eggs.

00:03:14.368 --> 00:03:19.249
The first, when the world around it is comfortable,
will be thin-shelled and quick to hatch.

00:03:19.249 --> 00:03:26.049
The second though, when the world around it
is less comfortable, will be hard-shelled—a cyst.

00:03:26.049 --> 00:03:31.488
And inside that cyst is a larva, fully-developed
but kept in wait until the conditions are

00:03:31.488 --> 00:03:33.709
okay for it to leave the cyst.

00:03:33.709 --> 00:03:39.598
With just the right mixture of salty water,
the Artemia will emerge from its rest.

00:03:39.598 --> 00:03:44.808
Scientists of course have done the obvious
thing and sent these cysts into space, launching

00:03:44.808 --> 00:03:50.159
them aboard the Apollo 16 and Apollo 17 moon
missions to assess their hardiness against

00:03:50.159 --> 00:03:55.609
cosmic radiation and many of the eggs were
able to hatch after that exposure, though

00:03:55.609 --> 00:03:57.709
with some deformities.

00:03:57.709 --> 00:04:02.149
But back here on earth, the hardiness of those
cysts makes them well-suited for the more

00:04:02.149 --> 00:04:04.389
mundane concerns of fish food.

00:04:04.389 --> 00:04:11.308
In the 1930s, a species called Artemia nauplii
became the live fish food of choice for many.

00:04:11.308 --> 00:04:14.609
And Artemia eggs continue to be sold for fish
cultivation.

00:04:14.609 --> 00:04:19.779
That’s actually how we got ours: James,
our master of microscopes, ordered a packet

00:04:19.779 --> 00:04:25.149
of about a million eggs on the internet and
then concocted his own special briny mixture

00:04:25.149 --> 00:04:31.309
like a mixologist at a microscopic bar to
give them the right conditions to emerge in.

00:04:31.309 --> 00:04:37.769
And Artemia could have remained largely just
a matter of fish food, except that in 1957,

00:04:37.769 --> 00:04:44.299
a man named Harold von Braunhut entered a
pet store and left with an idea.

00:04:44.299 --> 00:04:47.489
His muse… was a bucket full of fish food.

00:04:47.489 --> 00:04:48.919
You know, Artemia.

00:04:48.919 --> 00:04:54.199
This was a time when ant farms were popular,
and von Braunhut was struck with the notion

00:04:54.199 --> 00:04:58.699
that maybe what ant farms needed was an aquatic
twist.

00:04:58.699 --> 00:05:01.099
But there was a challenge.

00:05:01.099 --> 00:05:06.349
Hatching Artemia as fish food is not quite
the same as hatching Artemia as a pet.

00:05:06.349 --> 00:05:09.938
You only need the fish food to last as long
as it takes for the fish to eat it.

00:05:09.938 --> 00:05:14.629
But the Artemia that von Braunhut wanted to
sell as a pet only lasted a few days.

00:05:14.629 --> 00:05:20.789
So, as one would do, he recruited the help
of a marine biologist, a guy named Anthony

00:05:20.789 --> 00:05:26.390
D’Agostino to create a hardier Artemia,
though the secrets of how they did this, we

00:05:26.390 --> 00:05:28.699
have not been able to uncover.

00:05:28.699 --> 00:05:31.649
But the science alone is not enough to sell
shrimp.

00:05:31.649 --> 00:05:36.199
And von Braunhut was dealing with a customer
base that already had its heart broken by

00:05:36.199 --> 00:05:42.049
a similar product called the Instant Fish,
which used dormant African killifish eggs,

00:05:42.049 --> 00:05:45.338
but was unable to meet the demands of its
buyers.

00:05:45.338 --> 00:05:51.069
The thing to know about Harold von Braunhut
though was that his primary occupation was showman.

00:05:51.069 --> 00:05:56.648
He’d successfully sold a product called
Invisible Goldfish, which came with a fishbowl

00:05:56.648 --> 00:05:59.419
and fish food, but no fish.

00:05:59.419 --> 00:06:05.849
So, if you are a man who can sell no fish,
then you are definitely a man who can sell

00:06:05.849 --> 00:06:11.259
Sea Monkeys, and Von Braunhut was able to
do that by turning to comic book advertisements,

00:06:11.259 --> 00:06:21.099
and there, the kingdom of sea monkeys was
built .

00:06:21.099 --> 00:06:24.389
But the showmanship wasn’t just in the advertising.

00:06:24.389 --> 00:06:28.259
Every time a kid added the second packet in
their Instant Life kit, they were getting

00:06:28.259 --> 00:06:33.248
a bit of the same flair that made von Braunhut’s
other creations so thrilling.

00:06:33.248 --> 00:06:37.739
Because Packet 1, you know, the ozone that
was supposed to condition your water, and

00:06:37.739 --> 00:06:42.349
make it nice and homey for the second packet
of eggs that you’d add 24 hours later?

00:06:42.349 --> 00:06:46.079
Yeah, that packet wasn’t just water conditioner.

00:06:46.079 --> 00:06:48.359
It also had some eggs.

00:06:48.359 --> 00:06:52.459
And Packet 2—the one with the eggs—didn’t
just have eggs.

00:06:52.459 --> 00:06:56.429
It also had some water-soluble dye in it.

00:06:56.429 --> 00:07:00.049
And by the time you added that packet, the
first round of eggs would already have been

00:07:00.049 --> 00:07:07.109
hatched, but they would be difficult to see…until
the water filled with dye and made their hatched bodies visible.

00:07:07.109 --> 00:07:13.278
And the result, as the patent says, is “the
impression of instant life.”

00:07:13.278 --> 00:07:18.429
We should warn you now that the history of
the sea monkey only gets darker from here.

00:07:18.429 --> 00:07:24.199
Harold von Braunhut was a white supremacist
who, when he had to go see the U.S. attorney

00:07:24.199 --> 00:07:29.539
after lending a member of the Ku Klux Klan
money to buy illegal guns, did bring some

00:07:29.539 --> 00:07:31.309
sea monkeys with him.

00:07:31.309 --> 00:07:35.979
And because of this association, comic book
companies stopped advertising his creations,

00:07:35.979 --> 00:07:40.879
but von Braunhut was still able to sell his
sea monkeys in other ways.

00:07:40.879 --> 00:07:45.449
After his death in 2007, von Braunhut’s
widow signed a deal with a toy company to

00:07:45.449 --> 00:07:49.958
sell sea monkeys, only to later sue them for
breach of contract and trade infringement.

00:07:49.958 --> 00:07:54.739
The toy company claimed they were actually
sourcing different Artemia now, and while

00:07:54.739 --> 00:08:05.179
the case was settled, we do not know what
the terms were of this sea monkey settlement.

00:08:05.179 --> 00:08:10.639
Harold von Braunhut created a story around
Artemia that created an entirely different

00:08:10.639 --> 00:08:15.129
creature, to turn their most remarkable accomplishment
into a sleight of hand.

00:08:15.129 --> 00:08:20.179
And the showmanship was certainly clever,
allowing us to imagine that there are tiny

00:08:20.179 --> 00:08:24.479
individuals living tiny lives in tiny worlds
like ours.

00:08:24.479 --> 00:08:27.659
But of course, that is not the life of the
brine shrimp.

00:08:27.659 --> 00:08:28.929
Nor does it need to be.

00:08:28.929 --> 00:08:35.499
The sea monkey’s less contrived story is
out there, not frozen in time, but simply

00:08:35.499 --> 00:08:41.499
unfolding in millions of different directions
over tens of millions of years.

00:08:41.499 --> 00:08:48.109
Artemia, you were once a sea monkey to me,
but now I know the truth...you are you, which

00:08:48.109 --> 00:08:54.749
is a much better thing to know.

00:08:54.749 --> 00:09:01.049
Thank you for coming on this extra surprising
journey with us as we explore the unseen world

00:09:01.049 --> 00:09:02.249
that surrounds us.

00:09:02.249 --> 00:09:05.729
Don’t forget, you’ve only got one day
to pre-order that tardigrade t-shirt in the

00:09:05.729 --> 00:09:08.729
color of your choice before one of them goes
away forever.

00:09:08.729 --> 00:09:14.349
So, visit dftba.com or click the link in the
description if you’d like to order one of those.

00:09:14.349 --> 00:09:18.649
Hey, the people on our screen right now, they
are our Patreon patrons.

00:09:18.649 --> 00:09:24.629
The ones that allow us to look at the microcosmos
and say, “What stories could this tell about

00:09:24.629 --> 00:09:26.159
our world?

00:09:26.159 --> 00:09:31.429
And how surprised are we going to be when
we start uncovering those details?”

00:09:31.429 --> 00:09:33.379
We could not do that work without these people.

00:09:33.379 --> 00:09:36.379
So, if you like what we do, they are the people
to thank.

00:09:36.379 --> 00:09:39.739
And I also like what we do so I would like
to say thank you to them.

00:09:39.739 --> 00:09:43.209
If you want to see more from our Master of
Microscopes James Weiss, you can check out

00:09:43.209 --> 00:09:44.979
Jam &amp; Germs on Instagram.

00:09:44.979 --> 00:09:50.439
And if you want to see more from us, I bet
you there’s a subscribe button somewhere nearby.

